8 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Variance of a Sum or Difference of Independent Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162068 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162068 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0e3dad37-612c-56f1-a571-360fafe336b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162196 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162196 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162196 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162196 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162196 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162196 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162196 #edb/ref 1045 "Variance of Sums of Independent Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162196 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186162916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162196 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162196 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162196 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "402c5791-a328-536f-b52e-96d65b27ede7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162277 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186158864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162277 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109099" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162277 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162277 #edb/ref 1071 "The probability mass function of the independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$f(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.75$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n Find $\\text{Var}[3X+2Y].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162277 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "50853964-7ea7-5098-a333-b9b8507d9ac8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162277 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162277 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y].\n$$\n\nAlso, the variance of $X$ is determined by the formula \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{X}$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot f(x) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.2+2\\cdot 0.3+3\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =2.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.2+4\\cdot 0.3+9\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =5.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =5.9-{2.3}^{2} \\\\  & =0.61.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{Y}$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot f(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.25+2\\cdot 0.75 \\\\  & =1.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.25+4\\cdot 0.75 \\\\  & =3.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =3.25-{1.75}^{2} \\\\  & =0.1875.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[3X-2Y] & ={3}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{2}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y] \\\\  & ={3}^{2}(0.61)+{2}^{2}(0.1875) \\\\  & =6.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162916 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162916 #edb/ref 1061 31412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162916 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "85c48a68-bb08-5e36-af92-8eb493ace0d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162916 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162981 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162981 #edb/ref 1061 33995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162981 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0a0041f4-e6ca-5360-9d1e-8b7a07a7bccd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162981 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162992 #edb/ref 1061 34081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162992 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "24790bde-943d-5850-8dad-fcae56637f85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162992 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162994 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162994 #edb/ref 1061 34246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162994 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d8885fb1-6c97-579e-9795-07aef731fb3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162994 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163002 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163002 #edb/ref 1061 34354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163002 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bcaba5d6-1140-5eb4-b9e1-ea74caf408c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163002 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163250 #edb/ref 1064 "If $X$ and $Y$ are *independent* random variables, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}{[X+Y]} = \\textrm{Var}[X]+ \\textrm{Var}[Y] ,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}{[X-Y]} = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y]  .\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** When computing the variance of a *difference* of independent random variables, we *add* the variances!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163250 #edb/ref 1065 5023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163250 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3c595d74-9638-52f2-a4af-79dfe2ded69c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163250 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163284 #edb/ref 1064 "We have been using the fact that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}{[X+Y]} = \\textrm{Var}[X]+ \\textrm{Var}[Y]  .\n$$\n\nUsing the property that $\\textrm{Var}[aX] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X],$ we can show that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}{[aX+bY]} = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X]+ b^2\\textrm{Var}[Y] ,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nFor example, for two independent random variables $X$ and $Y$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[4X+3Y] & ={4}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{3}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y] \\\\  & =16\\text{Var}[X]+9\\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnce again, we need to show caution when considering a difference. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[4X-3Y] & =\\text{Var}[4X+(-3)Y] \\\\  & ={4}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+(-3)^{2}\\text{Var}[Y] \\\\  & =16\\text{Var}[X]+9\\text{Var}[Y].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMore generally, if the random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n,$ are *mutually independent*, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}\\left[\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i X_i\\right] = \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2\\textrm{Var}[X_i].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163284 #edb/ref 1065 5514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163284 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d56df0e7-9547-5cad-8697-f7c9d96ff12a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163284 #edb/ref 1067 "The Variance of a Sum of Scaled Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444625 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186444654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444625 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186444655 #edb/ref 175921860